ut specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {{\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {{\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            }},\n            {{\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }}\n        ]\n    }}\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {{\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        }},\n        {{\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        }},\n        {{\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        }},\n        {{\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }}\n    ]\n}}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n----Reports-----\ncommunity,full_content\r\n402,\"# Eating Disorders Community: Anorexia Nervosa and Bulimia Nervosa\n\nThe community focuses on the complex interrelations between various eating disorders, particularly Anorexia Nervosa and Bulimia Nervosa, along with their medical implications, treatment approaches, and the psychological factors influencing these conditions. Key entities include patients, medical treatments, and diagnostic criteria, highlighting the multifaceted nature of eating disorders and their impact on health.\n\n## Anorexia Nervosa as a critical health issue\n\nAnorexia Nervosa is a severe eating disorder characterized by self-imposed starvation and excessive weight loss, leading to significant health complications such as malnutrition, osteoporosis, and hormonal imbalances. The disorder poses complex medical and ethical challenges, particularly regarding treatment and patient autonomy, especially in detention settings. Patients often require specialized nutritional rehabilitation and may face severe health risks if not treated promptly. [Data: Entities (2028); Relationships (477, 949, 7270, 8697, 8710, +more)]\n\n## Bulimia Nervosa's treatment complexities\n\nBulimia Nervosa involves recurrent binge eating followed by compensatory behaviors, which can lead to serious health complications. Treatment typically requires a multifaceted approach, including psychotherapy and medication, to address both psychological and physical aspects of the disorder. The overlap between Bulimia Nervosa and Anorexia Nervosa complicates diagnosis and treatment, necessitating careful assessment and tailored interventions. [Data: Entities (4970); Relationships (519, 1054, 14390, 10060, +more)]\n\n## The role of Fluoxetine in treatment\n\nFluoxetine, an antidepressant, is commonly prescribed as part of treatment plans for eating disorders, particularly for reducing binge eating and purging behaviors in Bulimia Nervosa. However, it is important to monitor for potential side effects, such as the development of Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone secretion (SIADH), especially in older patients. This highlights the need for careful management of medication in patients with eating disorders. [Data: Entities (4027); Relationships (3239, 12962, 10062, +more)]\n\n## Gastroparesis as a complication\n\nGastroparesis is a condition that can complicate the treatment of patients with Anorexia Nervosa, affecting their ability to digest food properly. This condition is characterized by delayed gastric emptying, which can lead to significant weight loss and nutritional deficiencies. Effective management of gastroparesis is crucial for the overall treatment of patients with eating disorders, as it directly impacts their nutritional rehabilitation efforts. [Data: Entities (8898); Relationships (3494, 2858, 7280, +more)]\n\n## The importance of nutritional support\n\nNutritional support is critical in the treatment of Anorexia Nervosa, as patients often experience severe malnutrition due to self-imposed starvation. Specialized nutritional rehabilitation is necessary to address their nutritional deficiencies and promote recovery. In many cases, enteral nutrition may be required to ensure adequate nutrient intake, highlighting the importance of a comprehensive approach to managing eating disorders. [Data: Relationships (831, 477, 949, +more)]\n\n## Psychological factors influencing eating disorders\n\nEating disorders are significantly influenced by psychological factors, including body image issues and societal pressures. Groups such as models and athletes are at higher risk for developing these disorders due to the pressures to maintain specific body types. Understanding these psychological influences is essential for developing effective prevention and treatment strategies for eating disorders. [Data: Entities (8880, 8882, 8881, 8886, +more); Relationships (8708, 8709, 8712, +more)]\n\n## Diagnostic criteria and the role of DSM-5\n\nThe DSM-5 provides standardized criteria for diagnosing eating disorders, including Anorexia Nervosa and Bulimia Nervosa. This manual is crucial for mental health professionals as it reflects the latest research and understanding of these conditions, ensuring accurate diagnosis and treatment planning. The updates in the DSM-5 enhance the relevance of diagnostic criteria in clinical practice. [Data: Entities (4966); Relationships (8698, 8700, 8714, +more)]\"\r\n404,\"# Diabetes Management and Related Health Risks\n\nThe community focuses on various entities related to diabetes management, including glucemia, diabetes types, nutritional supplements, and associated health risks. These entities are interconnected through their roles in diagnosing, managing, and understanding diabetes and its complications, highlighting the importance of monitoring and nutritional support in diabetes care.\n\n## Glucemia as a critical health parameter\n\nGlucemia, or blood glucose levels, is a vital indicator in the management and diagnosis of diabetes. Regular monitoring of glucemia is essential for patients with diabetes, as it directly influences treatment decisions and health outcomes. For instance, glucemia levels are monitored closely in patients undergoing nutritional therapy and those with conditions like MELAS syndrome, where glucose management is crucial for overall health. The relationship between glucemia and diabetes management underscores the need for continuous monitoring to prevent complications such as hypoglycemia and hyperglycemia [Data: Entities (2139); Relationships (3184, 4661, 490, 1631, 5731, +more)].\n\n## Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 prevalence and management\n\nDiabetes Mellitus Type 2 (DM2) is a chronic condition that significantly impacts public health, particularly in regions like Spain, where its prevalence is rising. The management of DM2 often involves lifestyle changes, including diet and exercise, and may require medication. The condition is closely linked to obesity, highlighting the importance of weight management in its prevention and treatment. Furthermore, the American Diabetes Association provides guidelines for the diagnosis and management of DM2, emphasizing the need for comprehensive care strategies [Data: Entities (4094); Relationships (8113, 4113, 9693, 13101, +more)].\n\n## Nutritional supplements in diabetes care\n\nNutritional supplements, such as vitamin supplements and specific dietary formulas, play a supportive role in the management of diabetes. These supplements are particularly beneficial for patients with diabetes who may have specific nutritional deficiencies. For example, vitamin supplements are often recommended for cancer patients, and specific nutritional formulas are designed to improve metabolic control in diabetic patients. However, the necessity and effectiveness of these supplements in the absence of deficiencies remain debated [Data: Entities (6670, 6734); Relationships (805, 5733, 13108, +more)].\n\n## Complications associated with diabetes\n\nDiabetes is associated with various complications, including diabetic neuropathy, retinopathy, and cardiovascular diseases. These complications arise from prolonged hyperglycemia and require careful management to prevent progression. For instance, diabetic neuropathy can increase the risk of heart disease, while retinopathy can lead to vision loss. The interconnectedness of these conditions emphasizes the need for comprehensive diabetes management strategies that address both glycemic control and the prevention of complications [Data: Entities (4250, 4227, 4254); Relationships (12872, 13100, 13090, +more)].\n\n## The role of lifestyle factors in diabetes risk\n\nLifestyle factors such as obesity, sedentary behavior, and smoking significantly influence the risk of developing diabetes, particularly Type 2 diabetes. Sedentary lifestyles are recognized as a risk factor for various health issues, including diabetes and cardiovascular disease. The Mediterranean diet is often recommended to manage excess weight and reduce cardiovascular risk, highlighting the importance of dietary interventions in diabetes prevention and management [Data: Entities (7341, 7342, 7343); Relationships (11516, 13142, 13096, +more)].\"\r\n397,\"# Anorexia Nervosa and Nutritional Rehabilitation Community\n\nThis community encompasses key entities involved in the understanding, treatment, and management of Anorexia Nervosa, including professional organizations, research journals, and treatment protocols. The relationships among these entities highlight the importance of nutritional guidelines and interventions in addressing the complexities of this eating disorder.\n\n## Role of the American Dietetic Association\n\nThe American Dietetic Association (ADA) is a pivotal organization in the community, providing essential guidelines and recommendations for the nutritional management of eating disorders, particularly Anorexia Nervosa. The ADA's comprehensive resources inform dietitians and nutritionists on best practices for dietary interventions, which are crucial for the recovery of individuals suffering from this disorder. Their guidelines address various aspects of nutrition, including dietary practices for specific populations such as older adults and patients with conditions like dysphagia. The ADA's influence extends to the treatment of Anorexia Nervosa, where it emphasizes the importance of tailored nutritional interventions to restore health and support recovery [Data: Entities (793); Relationships (5080, 5082)].\n\n## Interconnection between Anorexia Nervosa and Osteoporosis\n\nAnorexia Nervosa is closely linked to an increased risk of developing osteoporosis, a condition characterized by weakened bones. This relationship is primarily due to the nutritional deficiencies and low body weight associated with Anorexia Nervosa, which can lead to significant bone health issues. Research indicates that individuals with Anorexia Nervosa often experience low bone mass and hormonal changes that exacerbate the risk of osteoporosis. Understanding this connection is vital for developing effective treatment protocols that address both the eating disorder and its physical health consequences [Data: Entities (8621); Relationships (11008)].\n\n## Importance of Nutritional Rehabilitation\n\nNutritional rehabilitation is a critical intervention for individuals with Anorexia Nervosa, focusing on restoring nutritional health and facilitating recovery. This approach is essential for addressing the severe malnutrition and weight loss that characterize the disorder. Nutritional rehabilitation aims to promote weight recovery and restore menstrual function, which are key components of the recovery process. The guidelines provided by organizations like the ADA play a significant role in shaping effective nutritional rehabilitation strategies, ensuring that patients receive the necessary support to regain their health [Data: Entities (8920); Relationships (17228)].\n\n## Refeeding Syndrome as a Treatment Concern\n\nRefeeding syndrome is a potentially life-threatening condition that can occur when feeding is resumed in malnourished individuals, particularly those recovering from Anorexia Nervosa. This syndrome underscores the need for careful management and monitoring during the refeeding process, as it poses significant risks to patients who have been in a state of starvation. Understanding the implications of refeeding syndrome is crucial for healthcare providers involved in the treatment of Anorexia Nervosa, as it highlights the complexities of nutritional rehabilitation and the importance of a structured approach to reintroducing nutrition [Data: Entities (8955); Relationships (17234)].\n\n## Research Contributions from Key Individuals\n\nSeveral researchers, including Miller KK and Grinspoon S, have made significant contributions to the understanding and treatment of Anorexia Nervosa. Their research focuses on various aspects of the disorder, including its effects on bone density and the endocrine system. The findings from these studies are essential for informing clinical practices and developing effective treatment protocols for individuals with Anorexia Nervosa. The collaboration between researchers and organizations like the American Psychiatric Association further enhances the community's knowledge base and treatment strategies [Data: Entities (8956, 8957); Relationships (17235, 17236)].\"\r\n399,\"# Diabetes and Eating Disorders Community\n\nThe community focuses on the intersection of Diabetes Mellitus Type 1 and various eating disorders, highlighting the complex relationships between these health conditions and the organizations involved in their management and research. Key entities include healthcare professionals, organizations providing support and guidelines, and the specific health conditions themselves.\n\n## Diabetes Mellitus Type 1 as a central health issue\n\nDiabetes Mellitus Type 1 (DM1) is a chronic autoimmune disease that significantly impacts individuals' health, particularly in managing insulin levels and blood glucose. The condition is prevalent among young individuals and can lead to various complications, including other autoimmune diseases and eating disorders. Effective management of DM1 is crucial to prevent serious health issues, making it a central focus of this community. [Data: Entities (4234); Relationships (3428, 8703, 13191, 13107, 13384, +more)]\n\n## The link between diabetes and eating disorders\n\nIndividuals with Diabetes Mellitus Type 1 are at an increased risk of developing eating disorders, such as Anorexia Nervosa and Bulimia Nervosa, due to the pressures of managing their weight and insulin intake. This relationship highlights the psychological challenges faced by patients and the need for integrated care approaches that address both diabetes management and mental health. [Data: Relationships (8704, 13387, 13385, 13084, 14389, +more)]\n\n## Role of Fundación SED in diabetes support\n\nThe Fundación SED plays a vital role in providing data and support for individuals with diabetes in Spain, particularly focusing on the prevalence of diabetes among minors. This organization is crucial for raising awareness and offering resources to help manage diabetes effectively, especially in the context of associated eating disorders. [Data: Entities (6784); Relationships (229, 13386)]\n\n## Comité Olímpico Internacional's guidelines for athletes\n\nThe Comité Olímpico Internacional (COI) provides essential guidelines for athletes with diabetes, particularly regarding protein intake and overall nutrition. These recommendations are critical for athletes managing Diabetes Mellitus Type 1, as they help optimize performance while ensuring health is not compromised. [Data: Entities (9333); Relationships (13107, 13396)]\n\n## Dra. Ana Isabel Sánchez Marcos's expertise\n\nDra. Ana Isabel Sánchez Marcos is a specialist in endocrinology and nutrition, focusing on the intersection of diabetes and eating disorders. Her work at the Complejo Asistencial Universitario de Salamanca is significant for providing specialized care to patients, highlighting the importance of expert guidance in managing these complex health issues. [Data: Entities (6779, 6365); Relationships (13083, 15679)]\n\n## Complications associated with Diabetes Mellitus Type 1\n\nPatients with Diabetes Mellitus Type 1 are at risk of developing various complications, including diabetic retinopathy and diabetic neuropathy. These complications can significantly affect quality of life and require ongoing management, underscoring the importance of comprehensive care strategies that address both diabetes and its associated risks. [Data: Entities (7210, 7211); Relationships (13382, 13383)]\"\r\n405,\"# Vitamins and Dietary Supplements Community\n\nThe community is centered around various vitamins and dietary supplements, including their roles in health, nutrition, and specific medical conditions. Key entities include vitamins, dietary supplements like Pharmaton Complex and Fisiogen Ferro, and their relationships with health conditions such as diabetes and bariatric surgery.\n\n## Vitamins as essential nutrients\n\nVitamins are crucial organic compounds necessary for normal growth and nutrition, particularly in specific health contexts such as after bariatric surgery or for individuals with diabetes. They play a vital role in maintaining health and preventing deficiencies, especially in vulnerable populations like the elderly and critically ill patients. The importance of vitamins is underscored by their inclusion in dietary reference intakes, which guide nutritional needs across different age groups [Data: Entities (2359); Relationships (9668, 9671, 9672, 9674, 9675, +more)].\n\n## Pharmaton Complex and its health benefits\n\nPharmaton Complex is a dietary supplement that combines vitamins, minerals, and ginseng, aimed at supporting overall health. Its formulation highlights the community's focus on comprehensive health solutions, particularly for individuals needing nutritional support. The relationship between Pharmaton Complex and other supplements indicates a shared goal of enhancing health through combined nutrient intake [Data: Entities (16918); Relationships (9678, 20756, 20754, +more)].\n\n## Role of Fisiogen Ferro in iron supplementation\n\nFisiogen Ferro is a company that produces iron supplements, which are often combined with vitamins to enhance health benefits. This relationship emphasizes the importance of iron in conjunction with vitamins, particularly for individuals suffering from anemia or those requiring additional iron intake. The interconnectedness of Fisiogen Ferro with other vitamin products illustrates the community's focus on addressing specific health needs through dietary supplementation [Data: Entities (16908); Relationships (9674, 20749, 18288, +more)].\n\n## Impact of vitamins on diabetes management\n\nCertain vitamins may need to be supplemented in individuals with diabetes, particularly the elderly, to address potential deficiencies. This relationship highlights the critical role of vitamins in managing chronic health conditions and the necessity for tailored nutritional support. The community's focus on vitamins in the context of diabetes underscores the importance of proper nutrient intake for maintaining health in affected individuals [Data: Relationships (1628)].\n\n## Interactions between vitamins and anticoagulants\n\nLipid-soluble vitamins can interact with anticoagulants, potentially affecting their action. This relationship is significant for patients on anticoagulant therapy, as it necessitates careful management of vitamin intake to avoid adverse effects. The community's awareness of these interactions is crucial for ensuring patient safety and effective treatment outcomes [Data: Relationships (9671, 19853)].\n\n## Nutritional support for critically ill patients\n\nVitamins are included in discussions about nutritional support for critically ill patients, emphasizing their potential benefits in recovery and health maintenance. This aspect of the community highlights the importance of tailored nutritional strategies in clinical settings, where specific vitamin supplementation can significantly impact patient outcomes [Data: Relationships (3766, 2419)].\n\n## Diversity of dietary supplements\n\nThe community encompasses a diverse range of dietary supplements, including multivitamins like Multicentrum and specialized products like Lunafem. This diversity reflects the varying health needs of consumers and the market's response to those needs through targeted supplement formulations. The relationships among these products indicate a competitive landscape focused on providing comprehensive health solutions [Data: Entities (16914, 16912); Relationships (20752, 20753)].\"\r\n396,\"# TCA and Diabetes Management Community\n\nThis community centers around the complex interplay between Trastornos de la Conducta Alimentaria (TCA) and diabetes management, particularly in patients with Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus (DM1). Key entities include patients struggling with TCA, their family histories, and the medical support systems involved in their care.\n\n## TCA's impact on diabetes management\n\nTrastornos de la Conducta Alimentaria (TCA) significantly complicates the management of diabetes, particularly for patients with Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus (DM1). The relationship between TCA and diabetes is critical, as patients may exhibit restrictive eating and purging behaviors that can lead to poor metabolic control. This necessitates comprehensive care strategies that address both the psychological and physical health needs of the patient. The severity of TCA may even require involuntary treatment to ensure patient safety, highlighting the urgent need for effective intervention strategies. [Data: Entities (6794, 6790); Relationships (3254, 13085)]\n\n## Family history and genetic predisposition\n\nThe family history of diabetes, particularly Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (DM2) in the patient's grandmother, indicates a genetic predisposition that may affect the patient's health outcomes. This familial link underscores the importance of considering genetic factors in the management of diabetes and associated eating disorders. Understanding the patient's family medical history can provide insights into potential health risks and inform tailored treatment approaches. [Data: Entities (6790, 6793); Relationships (13246, 16069)]\n\n## Role of multidisciplinary care\n\nA multidisciplinary team is essential in addressing the challenges posed by TCA in patients with diabetes. This team typically includes endocrinologists, nutritionists, and mental health professionals who collaborate to create a comprehensive care plan. Such an approach is vital for managing the complex needs of patients, ensuring that both their physical and psychological health are prioritized. The effectiveness of this team-based care model can significantly influence patient outcomes and quality of life. [Data: Entities (6794, 6791); Relationships (5500)]\n\n## Psychological factors influencing TCA\n\nPsychological factors, such as low self-esteem, are significant risk factors for the development of TCA. Many patients with eating disorders also struggle with issues related to self-worth, which can exacerbate their eating behaviors and complicate diabetes management. Addressing these psychological aspects is crucial for effective treatment, as improving self-esteem may help mitigate some of the behaviors associated with TCA. [Data: Entities (8883, 6790); Relationships (16076)]\n\n## Nutritional screening as a critical intervention\n\nNutritional screening is a vital component of care for patients with TCA, particularly those managing diabetes. This process helps assess the risk of malnutrition and informs dietary interventions tailored to the patient's specific needs. Given the restrictive eating behaviors often associated with TCA, regular nutritional assessments can play a crucial role in preventing complications and ensuring adequate nutritional intake. [Data: Entities (6794); Relationships (3692)]\"\r\n\n\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n4193,GROWTH HORMONE,\"Growth Hormone (GH) is a crucial hormone that plays a significant role in growth, cell reproduction, and regeneration, as well as influencing glucose metabolism. In patients with Anorexia Nervosa, GH levels are found to be elevated; however, this is accompanied by a decrease in Insulin-like Growth Factor 1 (IGF-1) due to resistance to GH. Additionally, GH levels may be diminished in individuals with liver disease, which can negatively impact metabolism and muscle mass. Furthermore, GH is being explored as a potential anti-aging treatment for older adults, although it is not an approved indication and carries various effects and risks. Overall, Growth Hormone is integral to several physiological processes, but its levels and effects can vary significantly depending on health conditions and age.\",6\r\n4237,DIABETIC RETINOPATHY,\"Diabetic retinopathy is a complication of diabetes that significantly impacts the eyes. It is characterized by damage to the retina, which occurs as a result of prolonged high blood sugar levels. This condition can lead to vision impairment and, if left untreated, may result in blindness. Additionally, diabetic retinopathy can worsen with rapid improvements in glycemic control, highlighting the importance of careful management of blood sugar levels in individuals with diabetes.\",12\r\n8726,HOSPITAL GENERAL UNIVERSITARIO GREGORIO MARAÑÓN,\"Hospital General Universitario Gregorio Marañón is a prominent hospital located in Madrid, known for its specialization in clinical nutrition and dietetics. The hospital plays a significant role in the treatment of eating disorders, providing comprehensive care and support for patients facing these challenges.\",12\r\n9468,NUTRICIÓN ADECUADA,The provision of appropriate nutrition to ensure proper growth and development in patients with Glycogen Storage Disease I.,2\r\n8724,ANA ZUGASTI,Ana Zugasti is one of the authors associated with the clinical nutrition and dietetics unit at Hospital General Universitario Gregorio Marañón in Madrid.,1\r\n8722,CYNTHIA GONZÁLEZ-ANTIGÜEDAD,Cynthia González-Antigüedad is one of the authors associated with the clinical nutrition and dietetics unit at Hospital General Universitario Gregorio Marañón in Madrid.,1\r\n8875,M.ª CRISTINA CUERDA COMPÉS,\"Adjunct physician at the Clinical Nutrition and Dietetics Unit at Hospital General Universitario Gregorio Marañón, specializing in nutritional support for eating disorders.\",1\r\n8876,MARTA MOTILLA DE LA CÁMARA,\"Adjunct physician at the Clinical Nutrition and Dietetics Unit at Hospital General Universitario Gregorio Marañón, focusing on nutritional therapy.\",1\r\n8877,LOREDANA ARHIP,\"Dietitian-Nutritionist at the Clinical Nutrition and Dietetics Unit at Hospital General Universitario Gregorio Marañón, providing dietary support for patients.\",1\r\n8721,IRENE BRETÓN,Irene Bretón is one of the authors 